JWT is URL safe token used to securely transmit info b/n parties.

Its is:
1. Digitally Signed-> Cannot be tampering with.
2. Stateless -> Sever does not store session data
3. Self-contained ->  Contains user identity + permissions

Use case:
Authenticate it with given credentials and generate token
Authorization: you can access protected apis/urls/services based on the user has an authorization. ex: admin/user
JWT is stateless that means it won't store state/data in server side.

flow:
1. User logs in -> server verifies credentials
2. Server creates a JWT and returns it.
3. Client stores token (localStorage/cookie).
4. Client send JWT to every request -> Authorization: Bearer <token>
5. Server verifies JWT -> if valid -> give access.

jwt has 3 parts separated by 3 dots: header.payload.algorithm
header.payload.signature

header-> contains metadata about token.
  {
   "alg" : "HS256",
    "typ" : "JWT"
  }

  Bse64URl-encoded

payload -> contains actual data (claims) very important.
common claims:
claim     meanings
sub      Subject(userID)
exp:     Expiration time
iat:     Issued at
roles:   User roles

ex:
{
 "sub" : "user123",
 "roles" : ["USER"],
 "iat" : 1710000000,
 "exp" : 1710003600
}
Also Bases64url-encoded.

Signature ->
{
  HMACSHA256(
  base64UrlEncode(header) + "." +base64UrlEncode(payload),secret)
}

purpose:
-> Ensures token was not modified
-> Ensures token came from your server.

JWT advantages:
* stateless
* Very fast for authorization
* Can be shared across services (microservices)
* Works with any front end (React, Angular mobile).

Note: jwt missunderstanding:
JWT is only signed, not encrypted. Anyone can read it's data -> just base64 decoded.
But they cannot modify it.
If sensitive data must be hidden -> use JWE (Encrypted JWT) or store only minimal claims.

JWT Code implementation:
we will build:
1. Login API  -> return JWT
2. Auth filter -> validated JWT
3. Protected endpoint -> requires JWT.

Java-Gradle-Springboot
step1: Add jwt dependencies in build.gradle
Step2: Add JWT utiity class where we add methods to generate token, validate token and extract user
In our code check: service.JwtService.java class.
Step3: add authentication filter class to check jwt token validity, extract user and set up SecurityContextHolder.
In our code check: security.JwtAuthFilter.java class.
step3: add configuration -> which url is permitted for example home page, and which urls such protected urls need authentication
and role based authorization (accees) to urls/apis/services. ex: user level access apis, admin level access apis.
step4: add login controller, verify credentials and authenticate if username and password available in db and correct
if not correct credentials -> 401, else will generate token
step5: add protected api controller. (it can accessbile with valid token only).

JWT Working flow in detailed:
we will follow the request path for:
1. Login Request
2. Protected API request.

1. Login flow:
When click on login button from UI, request will go to AuthController and loginMethod
-> It read credentials
-> Validate user (usually via service)
-> Call JWT service to generate token
-> Return token to client.
from controller --> service  (AuthController -> JwtService).
1.1 Controller calls JwtService
call generateToken(username)
JwtService responsibilities:
-> Create JWT with claims
-> Sign with JWT with secret key
-> Return token string
1.2 Controller return token : ResponseEntity.ok({token})
Client stores token in:
-> localStorage
-> cookies
->mobile secure storage
flow chart:
[client]
|username, password
[AuthController]
|
| generateToken(username)
|
[JwtService]
|
|return token -->
|
[AuthController]
|
|token
|
client

2. Authorized Request flow: (protected ui: ex: /profile)
Now client sends:
Get /profile
Authorization: Bearer <token>
This request passes through security filter
step1: Request enters spring security chain
Every request goes through:
OncePerRequestFilter -> UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter -> ....
in out code: check JwtAuthFilter.java class : is placed before UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.
step2: JwtAuthFilter runs first : doFilterInternal()
 -> extract the authorization header
 -> If token exists -> validate token
 -> if valid -> extract username
 -> create authentication object (auth) in our code.
 -> set authentication in SecurityContext
-> This makes spring think:
  -> user is logged in
  -> request is authenticated

 Simple example to understand authorization levels:
 we used to shopping in amazon: lets think about products.
 authorization roles: user/customer, admin/prodctmanager
 1. As a user i can see products page there i can view only means i cannot add or modify
 2. As a admin access, can modify product info like quantity, price and can add other products. this page only accessible with admin access only.

